  "raw_text": "Step 1: First, we need to find the measure of angle ACB. Since the sum of angles in a triangle is 180 degrees, we can calculate angle ACB as follows:\n\\[\n\\angle ACB = 180^\\circ - \\angle BAC - \\angle ABC = 180^\\circ - 54^\\circ - 52^\\circ = 74^\\circ\n\\]\n\nStep 2: Next, we use the fact that the point where the altitudes intersect (the orthocenter) forms a cyclic quadrilateral with the vertices of the triangle. This means that the opposite angles of this quadrilateral are supplementary. Specifically, for quadrilateral ABCH, we have:\n\\[\n\\angle AHB + \\angle ACB = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nStep 3: Substituting the value of angle ACB from Step 1 into the equation from Step 2, we get:\n\\[\n\\angle AHB + 74^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nStep 4: Solving for angle AHB, we find:\n\\[\n\\angle AHB = 180^\\circ - 74^\\circ = 106^\\circ\n\\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{106}<|im_end|>",
